Internal impingement in overhead athletes — Corpus and colleagues (@med-source-internal-impingement: 0, is #reference, #part-of @medicine-sources) { r_39ZZD5X2XJRC9834DBHREBSS9X
Keith T. Corpus, Christopher L. Camp, David M. Dines, David W. Altchek, and Joshua S. Dines. Evaluation and treatment of internal impingement of the shoulder in overhead athletes. World Journal of Orthopedics 7, 2016, pages 776–784. DOI 10.5312/wjo.v7.i12.776. English.

[Free full text](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5155252/).

For a focused mechanical question, read Introduction and Pathophysiology and Biomechanics, including the kinetic-chain and capsular discussions. Read Diagnosis only after the normal anatomy and clinical-reasoning guides. The population is overhead athletes, with particular loading histories and adaptations; the account must not be generalized into a diagnosis for everyone with pain during rotation.

For later critical study, read the entire review and trace a mechanistic claim to an original study in its references. The management discussion is a 2016 narrative review, to be compared with newer evidence. The article does not supply a personal exercise or surgical plan.

**Access and reuse:** the article states CC BY-NC 4.0; retain attribution and its noncommercial condition if reusing material. It remains an external reference here. [Licence](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/).

